  ],
  "hints": [
    "Baseline: for every starting index, walk right accumulating a sum until it reaches the target, and keep the shortest width you find.",
    "All values are non-negative, so adding an element only raises a window's sum and dropping the leftmost element only lowers it.",
    "Keep two pointers and one running sum. Advance right by one element, then while the sum is still at least the target and the window is not empty, record the current width and move left one step to the right."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The brute force fixes a left end i and lets the right end j run to the end of the array, keeping a running sum and recording the width of every window that reaches the target. There are n(n+1)/2 windows, so that is O(n\u00b2) additions. The optimal version keeps one window [left, right] plus a running sum: the right end advances one element at a time, and as soon as the window sum is at least the target the width is recorded and the window is then shrunk from the left while the sum still reaches the target and the window still has at least one element, so the shortest valid window ending at right is measured exactly. Both pointers only move forward, which is what makes the scan linear.",
    "why_optimal": "Each element is added to the running sum once and removed from it at most once, so the total work is O(n) and only four counters are stored. That is optimal because any correct solution must read every element at least once, and the answer itself needs no auxiliary structure. The shrinking step is safe only because the values are non-negative: removing the left end never increases the sum, so once the window falls below the target it cannot be rescued by extending to the right past its current position. At 200 elements the quadratic scan still costs roughly 20000 additions, so both implementations run instantly within the stated bounds.",
    "pitfalls": "The not-found case needs a sentinel larger than any legal window (n+1 works) mapped back to 0 at the end, because a counter initialised to 0 would also report 0 when the target is only just met. The width must be recorded inside the while loop before the subtractions, otherwise every recorded width is one too small. A target of 0 (or a zero-valued element) is legal input, and then the sum never drops below the target, so the shrink must stop when the window is about to become empty: a loop guarded only by the sum records width 0 for an empty window and reports 0 even though a single element already reaches the target. The left pointer must never move backwards, which breaks if the while loop is replaced by an inner loop that resets left for each right, turning the solution back into O(n\u00b2)."
  },
